# Swift Refactoring Guidelines

## File Organization and Namespacing
- When refactoring large files (>800 lines), create a dedicated directory structure (e.g., `Views/ComponentName/`)
- Use namespaces to prevent naming conflicts:
  ```swift
  enum Views_ComponentName {}
  extension Views_ComponentName {
    struct SubComponentView: View { ... }
  }
  ```
- Create proxy implementations with the original filename for backward compatibility
- Keep original file path but redirect to namespaced components

## API and Service Access
- Always implement fallback mechanisms for critical services:
  ```swift
  func getAPIKey() -> String? {
    // Try Keychain first
    if let key = keychainService.getAPIKey(...), !key.isEmpty {
      return key
    }
    
    // Fall back to UserDefaults (especially for simulator)
    if let key = UserDefaults.standard.string(forKey: "KeyName"), !key.isEmpty {
      print("Using key from UserDefaults (for simulator)")
      return key
    }
    
    return nil
  }
  ```
- Check both sources when verifying if a key/value exists


## File Organization
- Create dedicated folders for extracted components (e.g., `Views/Settings/`, `Views/Vault/`)
- Group related components in the same folder (e.g., all vault-related views in Views/Vault/)
- Keep file sizes under 800 lines of code
- Extract components from files exceeding 1000 lines
- Organize imports at the top of the file with system imports first, then project imports

## Component Extraction
- Extract components based on clear responsibilities (Single Responsibility Principle)
- Extract reusable UI elements into separate view components
- Extract complex sections (like headers, search fields, buttons) into their own components
- Move business logic from views into view models
- When extracting components, include proper documentation comments explaining their purpose

## View Structure
- Break down large views into smaller, more manageable subviews
- Use `@ObservedObject` for shared view models across components
- Use `@Binding` for properties that need to be modified by child views
- Pass only necessary properties to child components
- Keep View body methods under 100 lines

## State Management
- Centralize state in view models rather than views
- Minimize use of `@State` variables in parent views
- Pass view models to child components instead of individual properties
- Use environment objects for global state that many components need to access
- Be explicit about when state updates trigger UI refreshes

## Notification Handling
- Centralize notification observers in one place
- Use proper lifecycle management for observers (add in onAppear, remove in onDisappear)
- Use typed notification pattern when possible
- Consider using Combine publishers instead of NotificationCenter for more type safety

## Common UI Patterns
- Use DisclosureGroup for expandable sections
- Extract header/footer elements into separate components
- Create dedicated components for list items
- Place search functionality in dedicated components
- Keep alert and sheet presentation logic in the parent view

## Naming Conventions
- Use descriptive names that indicate component purpose
- Suffix view components with "View" (e.g., `VaultHeaderView`)
- Keep consistent naming across related components
- Use prefixes to group related components (e.g., `Vault` prefix for all vault-related views)
- Follow Apple's naming guidelines for Swift (camelCase for variables, methods; PascalCase for types)


## View Models

- Make view model properties injectable to support testing. Use `private(set) var` instead of `private let` for properties that need to be injected.
- Provide custom initializers to support dependency injection in tests.
- Example:
  ```swift
  class ViewModel: ObservableObject {
      private(set) var persistenceController: PersistenceController
      
      init() {
          self.persistenceController = PersistenceController.shared
          // Other initialization
      }
      
      init(persistenceController: PersistenceController) {
          self.persistenceController = persistenceController
          // Other initialization
      }
  }


## Testing
- Create separate mock classes with test prefixes (e.g., `TestComponentViewModel`) 
- Avoid naming conflicts by using unique names for test enums/structs
- For UI components, prefer synchronous testing over asynchronous when possible
- Initialize variables in mock classes that match the real implementation


## Testing & Mocks

- When mocking an object, decide whether to inherit from the original class or simply implement the same protocol/interface.
- If inheriting, ensure to properly override all required methods.
- If implementing an interface, ensure the mock implements all required methods.
- Keep test mocks separate from production code.
- When creating mocks, implement the minimal functionality needed for tests.
- Example mock:
  ```swift
  class MockDocumentViewModel: ObservableObject {
      var mockDocument: DocumentDetails?
      var updatedTitle: String = ""
      
      func loadDocument(id: UUID, completion: @escaping (DocumentDetails?) -> Void) {
          completion(mockDocument)
      }
  }


## Avoiding Common Errors
- Check for redeclarations across test and production code
- When migrating enums, ensure all references are updated
- Apply consistent styling patterns across refactored components
- Be cautious of "multiple commands produce X.stringsdata" errors when moving UI files


## SwiftUI Views

- When refactoring view initialization, ensure all call sites are updated
- Be cautious when adding or removing parameters to view initializers
- Document expected parameter usage, especially when changes might affect tests
- Keep view initializers consistent across related components
- Consider using view builders for complex view compositions

## Refactoring Process

- Start with the largest files first (>1000 lines)
- Identify logical components within large files (headers, search fields, list items, etc.)
- Extract one component at a time and test after each extraction
- Update all references to extracted components
- Check for duplicate functionality before creating new components
- Document the refactoring process and decisions made
- Create a detailed plan before starting major refactoring

## Error Prevention

- Check for missing imports in extracted files
- Verify property access levels (public, private, etc.)
- Ensure all required dependencies are passed to extracted components
- Test each component after extraction
- Look for patterns that could lead to memory leaks (strong reference cycles)
- Validate that component extraction doesn't break existing functionality

# Testing SwiftUI Components

### Test Mocking
- Use static swappable implementations for dependencies in production code to enable test mocking
  ```swift
  // In production code
  struct DocumentListItem {
      // Add a static property that can be swapped in tests
      static var isDocumentLockedImpl: (UUID) -> Bool = { documentId in
          DocumentLockManager.shared.isDocumentLocked(documentId)
      }
      
      var isLocked: Bool {
          return DocumentListItem.isDocumentLockedImpl(id)
      }
  }
  
  // In test code
  let originalImplementation = DocumentListItem.isDocumentLockedImpl
  DocumentListItem.isDocumentLockedImpl = { documentId in
      return documentId == mockedLockedId
  }
  // Test code...
  DocumentListItem.isDocumentLockedImpl = originalImplementation // Restore original
  ```

### ViewInspector Testing
- Add accessibility labels to UI components to make them easier to test with ViewInspector
  ```swift
  if document.isLocked {
      Image(systemName: "lock.fill")
          .foregroundColor(.blue)
          .accessibilityLabel("Locked")
  }
  ```

- Test UI behaviors rather than implementation details to reduce test fragility
  ```swift
  // Instead of looking for specific nested structure:
  let images = try unlockedTopHStack.findAll(ViewType.Image.self)
  let lockImages = images.filter { 
      (try? image.actualImage().name() == "lock.fill") ?? false 
  }
  XCTAssertEqual(lockImages.count, 0, "Lock icon should not be visible")
  ```

### Test Data Structures
- Create dedicated test data structures to simplify test setup
  ```swift
  // A test-specific version of the model
  struct TestDocumentListItem: Identifiable {
      let id: UUID
      let title: String
      let createdAt: Date
      let folderName: String?
      let tagNames: [String]
      let text: String?
      let isLocked: Bool
  }
  
  // Add convenience initializers to production types for testing
  extension DocumentRow {
      init(testDocument: TestDocumentListItem, ...) {
          // Implementation that creates real model from test model
      }
  }
  ```

### UI Testing Separation
- UI tests require separate test targets with proper setup
- When UI tests fail because of configuration issues, create placeholder unit tests that verify core logic until UI test infrastructure is properly configured

### State Management in Tests
- Always reset mock state in setUp() methods
- Carefully save and restore global state when tests complete
- Avoid side effects from one test affecting another

## Test Method Signature Consistency

- When overriding methods in test subclasses, ensure exact parameter name matching:
  ```swift
  // Parent class
  func deleteDocument(_ id: UUID) { ... }
  
  // Test subclass - CORRECT: same parameter syntax
  override func deleteDocument(_ id: UUID) { ... }
  
  // Test subclass - INCORRECT: different parameter label
  override func deleteDocument(withId id: UUID) { ... } // Will cause errors
  ```

- Add delegating methods to preserve backward compatibility when needed:
  ```swift
  // Add for backward compatibility
  func deleteDocument(withId id: UUID) {
      deleteDocument(id) // Call the properly overridden method
  }
  ```

## Mock Class Implementation

- Ensure mock classes properly override initializers from parent classes:
  ```swift
  // Parent class
  class PersistenceController {
      init() { ... }
  }
  
  // Mock subclass - correct initialization
  class MockPersistenceController: PersistenceController {
      override init() {
          super.init()
          // Additional setup
      }
  }
  ```

- When mocking CoreData entities, account for type conversion between domain models and view models:
  ```swift
  // Converting CoreData entities to view models
  func fetchDocuments() {
      let documents = mockController.fetchDocuments()
      
      self.documents = documents.compactMap { document -> DocumentListItem? in
          guard let id = document.id, let title = document.title else {
              return nil
          }
          
          return DocumentListItem(
              id: id,
              title: title,
              createdAt: document.createdAt ?? Date(),
              folderName: nil,
              tagNames: [],
              text: document.text
          )
      }
  }
  ```

## Test Plan Maintenance

- Maintain a comprehensive test plan with clear sections:
  1. Unit Tests - Model/ViewModel logic
  2. UI Tests - View appearance and interactions
  3. Integration Tests - Component interaction
  4. Performance Tests - Efficiency metrics
  
- Mark tests as completed in test plan as they are implemented:
  ```markdown
  - [x] `testDeleteDocument` - Test document deletion works correctly
  - [ ] `testOCRTextSearch` - Test searching within document OCR text
  ```
  
- Ensure test names in code match those in the test plan for clarity
- Add tests for edge cases that were discovered during refactoring

## Document Model Field Access

- When accessing CoreData entity fields, use consistent property names across the codebase:
  ```swift
  // Consistent property naming
  document.createdAt = date  // Not createdDate or dateCreated
  document.updatedAt = date  // Not lastModifiedDate or modifiedDate
  ```

- Include null checks for model fields that might be optional:
  ```swift
  guard let id = document.id, let title = document.title else {
      return nil
  }
  ```


